Hello and welcome here;

The "x1=3Dk2" style for argument slot marking is used for showing both the=
=20
slot number (here x1), and the etymology of that slot, or in other words=20
from which argument slot of which of the source words that have been=20
used to construct the lujvo.

In the case of samyzilkei, which is a lujvo coming from the tanru {skami=20
zi'o kelci}: "x1=3Dk2 is a video game on platform x2=3Ds1", we have the x1=
=20
slot marked with the etymological information "k2", for "the second slot=20
of the gismu that is used for building this lujvo and begins with the=20
letter K", so {kelci} here, and we have the x2 slot marked with "s1", so=20
the first slot of the source gismu beginning with S, so {skami} in this=20
case.
So the first slot of {samyzilkei} is a {se kelci}, thus a game, and the=20
second slot comes from {skami}, so a computer.

=E2=80=A2 http://jbovlaste.lojban.org/help/definitions.html

As for "tityzme: g1 is a melon of cultivar g2":
This notation is similar to the previous paradigm, but the slot number=20
information (x1=3D / x2=3D / x3=3D ...) is missing, which can be even more=
=20
confusing.

Personally, I like neither of those etymological paradigms, I prefer to=20
use the simple and clear x1/x2/x3/... notation, even for lujvo. I think=20
that if the etymology of the lujvo is deemed important, it should be put=20
in the notes or in a specific field, but not clutter the definition.

Many beginners have complained about those notations. Except the page=20
I've linked above, there doesn't seem to be an explanation of those=20
notations that is readily accessible and easy to find from the main page=20
of Jbovlaste.

mi'e la .ilmen. mu'o


